hare-lsp's overlay patch for stdlib hare::parse::parse.ha.

Applied by the Makefile's hare/parse/.stamp target on top of a fresh
copy of $(STDLIB)/hare/parse/parse.ha. The build wrapper tolerates
"already applied" results (`patch -N`) so the same patch works on
Hare stdlib versions that have since adopted the same fix upstream
(e.g. Alpine's hare=0.26.0.1-r0).

What it changes:
  Move `lex::unlex(lexer, tok);` from after the alternatives loop in
  want() to before it. This is the upstream fix; we carry the same
  diff here because the version of stdlib hare-lsp targets does not
  ship it yet.

Why:
  Stdlib uses mkloc(lexer) inside the loop and in the trailing
  syntaxerr() call. mkloc does an internal lex+unlex cycle, leaving a
  token in the lexer's single-slot unlex buffer. The lex::unlex(lexer,
  tok) call further down then asserts with "attempted to unlex more
  than one token" and aborts the process. That fires routinely on
  intermediate editing states (e.g. the user has just typed `let x =`
  and is still typing the right-hand side), so an unmodified stdlib
  parser crashes the LSP.

  Moving the unlex above the loop primes the unlex slot with `tok`
  before any mkloc call. Subsequent mkloc invocations then lex `tok`
  back out of the slot, unlex it again, and leave the slot holding
  `tok` - no clash.

To refresh for a new Hare release: same procedure as documented in
hare/parse/README.md.

Apply with: `patch -N -p1 -d hare/parse < hare/parse/parse.ha.patch`

--- a/parse.ha
+++ b/parse.ha
@@ -40,6 +40,7 @@

 	let buf = memio::dynamic();
 	defer io::close(&buf)!;
+	lex::unlex(lexer, tok);
 	for (let i = 0z; i < len(want); i += 1) {
 		const tstr = if (want[i] == ltok::NAME) "name"
 			else lex::tokstr((want[i], void, mkloc(lexer)));
@@ -48,7 +49,6 @@
 			fmt::fprint(&buf, ", ")!;
 		};
 	};
-	lex::unlex(lexer, tok);
 	return syntaxerr(mkloc(lexer), "Unexpected '{}', was expecting {}",
 		lex::tokstr(tok), memio::string(&buf)!);
 };
